      "writtenOn": "2021-02-10T09:03:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t know well how to fix this, but if I\u0027m reading this correctly, this gets called transitively by getLegacyLockdownState from filterNetworkInfo, which is called from binder threads.\n\nThat means it\u0027s possible that between l.5107 and l.5111 the default network has changed and this method returns the wrong result. In fact, it\u0027s also possible mLockdownEnabled changes between l.5117 and l.5123, and we return CONNECTING instead of the original state.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5104,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 5104,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "181f38dd5dc34af2dbc6c5a942dca3239f3e9733",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e764fad8_787d306a",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 37
      },
      "lineNbr": 5104,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-11T14:01:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, there may be some tearing while the default network is switching. The fact that the VPN\u0027s underlying network is static in legacyLockdownNai.declaredUnderlyingNetworks helps avoid that.\n\nI\u0027ve removed the check in 5117 because it was superfluous (already done in 5097 called by 5123), which also helps a bit, but I don\u0027t think there\u0027s a way to avoid tearing without blocking the handler thread.\n\nThat said, I don\u0027t think it\u0027s too bad. The tearing can only happen when this method is not called on the handler thread. So in particular, the broadcasts sent by LegacyTypeTracker should not be affected because they only run on the handler thread.\n\nI wrote up this comment:\n\n        // This method can be called both on any thread. When called from threads other than the\n        // handler thread (e.g., legacy connectivity APIs running on binder threads), it may return\n        // inconsistent state if, for example, the default network has changed in the meantime.\n\nbut I don\u0027t know if it\u0027s worth adding it, since pretty much any (non-trivial) synchronous API call has this problem.",
      "parentUuid": "8e5d3e69_5c68470b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5104,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 5104,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "181f38dd5dc34af2dbc6c5a942dca3239f3e9733",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9311efa2_c3b3b09c",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 37
      },
      "lineNbr": 5104,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-12T05:41:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think our synchronous methods have internal tearing generally. You certainly can\u0027t rely on getting consistent result between return values of two of them, or with the callbacks, but I don\u0027t think you can get, say, a NC that\u0027s teared halfway through states because we make defensive copies all the time. Well, maybe there is some of it some of the time that we missed. But this one seems a bit glaring. And it could be fixed with locking... but then that\u0027s what you want to remove.\n\nThat being said, I agree with you it\u0027s not necessarily too bad. IIUC, the followup patches move out the state that should kept synchronized with the lockdown boolean, making this a lot less prone to this kind of tearing – hopefully you may get a result that\u0027s locally wrong but not internally inconsistent.\n\nI gues what I\u0027m trying to say is : okay, fine",
      "parentUuid": "e764fad8_787d306a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 5104,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 5104,
        "endChar": 45
      },
